{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers","@type":"LeanModule","name":"FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.qns_unit_layers","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.qns_unit_layers","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/qns-unit-layers.lean#L97-L107","doc":"Resolving declaration for `lean.ref`\n`qou:QOU.FluidDynamics.qns_unit_layers`: the two-layer convention\nis well-formed — the conversions are mutually consistent and the\nphysical layer has fixed viscosity.  Conjoins the two structural\nfacts: `ν_phys = 1/(2q)` exactly, and the enstrophy weight\nconverts by `(q−1)²`. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:def:qns-unit-layers","label":"def:qns-unit-layers","md":"quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/qns-unit-layers.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.kPhys_sq_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.kPhys_sq_mul","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/qns-unit-layers.lean#L87-L93","doc":"The enstrophy conversion is definitionally the squared wavenumber\nconversion: `(k_phys)²·e = (q−1)²·(k_sub²·e)` — layer conversion\ncommutes with forming enstrophy densities. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.nu_phys_near_half","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.nu_phys_near_half","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/qns-unit-layers.lean#L66-L84","doc":"**The physical viscosity is within `(q−1)/2` of `1/2`**: for\n`q = 1 + ε` with `ε > 0`,\n`1/2 − ν_phys = ε/(2(1+ε)) ≤ ε/2` and `ν_phys < 1/2`.  So the\nphysical frame's viscosity converges to the fixed value `1/2` at\nrate `ε/2` — no vanishing-viscosity degeneration in this layer. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.nu_phys_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.nu_phys_eq","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/qns-unit-layers.lean#L58-L64","doc":"**The physical viscosity is exactly `1/(2q)`** — an algebraic\nidentity, not a limit: `c_q·ν_q = (1/(q−1))·((q−1)/(2q)) = 1/(2q)`.\nThe physical layer is a fixed-viscosity frame. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.omegaPhys","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.omegaPhys","kind":"def","line":53,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/qns-unit-layers.lean#L53-L56","doc":"Physical enstrophy weight: `Ω_phys = (q−1)²·Ω_sub` (the `k²`\nweight converts with the square of the wavenumber factor —\nthe `ε²` weight the SF5 probe reports). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.kPhys","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.kPhys","kind":"def","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/qns-unit-layers.lean#L49-L51","doc":"Physical wavenumber: `k_phys = (q−1)·k_sub` (lengths scale by\n`c_q`, so wavenumbers scale by `1/c_q = q−1`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.nuPhys","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.nuPhys","kind":"def","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/qns-unit-layers.lean#L44-L47","doc":"The physical-layer viscosity: `ν_phys = c_q · ν_q`\n(the `X = c_q·x`, `T = c_q·t` rescaling with velocity unrescaled\nmultiplies the viscosity by `c_q`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.nuSub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.nuSub","kind":"def","line":41,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/qns-unit-layers.lean#L41-L42","doc":"The substrate-layer q-ladder viscosity `ν_q = (q−1)/(2q)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.cQ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FluidDynamics.QnsUnitLayers.cQ","kind":"def","line":37,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/fluid-dynamics/qns-unit-layers.lean#L37-L39","doc":"The canonical substrate scale `c_q = 1/(q−1)` (the corpus's\nexisting derived quantity; the layer conversion factor). ","signatureChunk":0}]}